gm-method/rendering (a 'objects look like a different scene than the ground' lighting bug): when a scene composites a SEPARATE renderer's backdrop (e.g. a raymarched planet with its OWN sun) with engine-lit objects (MeshStandardMaterial trees/rocks/grass/models lit by a THREE DirectionalLight), the two suns drift apart and every object + its cast shadow is lit from a different direction than the terrain. ROOT to check: are the two sun directions wired to ONE source, or independently defined (a backdrop sunLocal default + a scene sunPosition default with no sync)? A workflow/grep for the sun symbols across both subsystems shows the gap. COMPOUNDING TRAP: a player-following shadow updater often REWRITES the light position every frame from a fixed offset constant -> any sunPosition config edit silently doesn't stick (the offset wins); the live light direction = normalize(-shadowOffset), not the config. FIX: make the shadow offset's DIRECTION the single source of truth - a setSunDirection(unitDir) that re-aims the offset along the backdrop's sun dir (preserve the offset LENGTH for shadow-camera framing), called after the backdrop builds. Then objects + shadows match the terrain. WITNESS: read the live DirectionalLight, compute normalize(position-target), dot it with the backdrop sun dir -> angle should be ~0deg (was tens of degrees). The fix is a one-vector wiring but touches deliberately-tuned shading/shadow framing, so witness before/after.